<div class="bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100">
  <.marketing_nav current_user={@current_user} current={:docs} />

  <section class="py-12 sm:py-16">
    <.docs_layout
      current="bridge-upgrades"
      updated="August 21, 2026"
      toc={[
        {"versions", "Find the version you run"},
        {"compatibility", "Compatibility status"},
        {"upgrade", "Upgrade a bridge"},
        {"rollback", "Return to a known-good release"}
      ]}
    >
      <.docs_header current="bridge-upgrades">
        <:dek>
          Move the <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-sm">emisar-mcp</code>
          bridge on each workstation to a new release.
        </:dek>
      </.docs_header>

      <p class="mt-6 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        To upgrade runners instead, see <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/runner-upgrades"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >Upgrade runners</.link>.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="versions">Find the version you run</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The
        <a
          href={~p"/app/agents"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >AI agents</a>
        page shows the bridge version each key last reported. A cloud client leaves this value
        blank because it has no bridge, and nothing to upgrade. On the workstation, run:
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="shell" copy_text="emisar-mcp --version"><span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> emisar-mcp --version</.docs_code>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        One workstation runs one bridge, shared by every client on it. You upgrade once per
        machine, not once per client.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="compatibility">Compatibility status</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Each reported version is compared against two published numbers: the minimum we still
        support, and the release we recommend you run.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-5 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-zinc-800">
        <table class="w-full min-w-[420px] text-left text-sm">
          <thead class="bg-zinc-900/60 text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-zinc-400">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3">Value</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3">Current setting</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-zinc-900 text-zinc-300">
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-zinc-100">Minimum</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3"><code>{Emisar.Compat.mcp_minimum()}</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-zinc-100">Recommended release</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3"><code>{Emisar.Compat.mcp_target()}</code></td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <ul class="mt-5 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">outdated</strong>
            is above the minimum but behind what we recommend. It keeps working. The console shows
            a version chip, and nothing else changes.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <.chip tone={:rose} icon="state.warning">unsupported</.chip>
            is below the minimum. The key shows this in place of its usual
            activity. It still connects today, but that is not a promise. Upgrade it.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The version is self-reported, so treat it as housekeeping, never a security control —
        what a bridge may do is decided by your policies and its key, not by the version it
        claims.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Upgrade your runners and bridges in the same pass. For which versions work together, see <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/compatibility"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >compatibility and deprecation</.link>.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="upgrade">Upgrade a bridge</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Run the installer on each workstation. It downloads the new binary next to the current
        one, checks the checksum and the version it reports, keeps a copy of the old binary, and
        only then swaps them. If any step fails, it puts everything back.
      </p>
      <.os_docs_code phx-no-format detected={@detected_os}>
        <:tab
          os={:linux}
          label="Linux"
          copy_text="curl -fsSL https://emisar.dev/install-mcp.sh | sudo bash"
        ><span class="text-zinc-500"># replaces the emisar-mcp binary in place</span>
<span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> curl -fsSL https://emisar.dev/install-mcp.sh | sudo bash

<span class="text-zinc-500"># confirm the new version</span>
<span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> emisar-mcp --version</:tab>
        <:tab
          os={:windows}
          label="Windows"
          copy_text="irm https://emisar.dev/install-mcp.ps1 | iex"
        ><span class="text-zinc-500">PS&gt;</span> irm https://emisar.dev/install-mcp.ps1 | iex

<span class="text-zinc-500"># confirm the new version</span>
<span class="text-zinc-500">PS&gt;</span> emisar-mcp --version</:tab>
        <:tab
          os={:macos}
          label="macOS"
          copy_text="curl -fsSL https://emisar.dev/install-mcp.sh | sudo bash"
        ><span class="text-zinc-500"># replaces the emisar-mcp binary in place</span>
<span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> curl -fsSL https://emisar.dev/install-mcp.sh | sudo bash

<span class="text-zinc-500"># confirm the new version</span>
<span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> emisar-mcp --version</:tab>
      </.os_docs_code>
      <ul class="mt-5 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Client config and API key are untouched.</strong>
            The installer replaces only the binary. An emisar entry already in a client config is
            left alone.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Every copy is replaced.</strong>
            On macOS and Linux the installer updates the bridge in both
            <.docs_inline_code>~/.local/bin</.docs_inline_code>
            and
            <.docs_inline_code>/usr/local/bin</.docs_inline_code>
            when it finds it in each, so no client keeps launching an old one. Windows keeps one
            copy, in <.docs_inline_code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\Programs\Emisar\bin</.docs_inline_code>.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">A running client keeps the binary it already loaded.</strong>
            The client starts the bridge as a child process, so replacing the file on disk does not
            change the one already running. Restart the client to pick up the new one.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Then check it from the client.</strong>
            Ask the agent to list your runners. An answer means the client is running the new
            bridge — the version command only checks the binary on disk.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <.link navigate={~p"/docs/agents-and-keys"} class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300">
          Manage agents & keys
        </.link>
        owns the bridge's key handling and its version chip.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="rollback">Return to a known-good release</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Going back is the same installer with the version you were on. Restart the client and
        check it the same way.
      </p>
      <.os_docs_code phx-no-format detected={@detected_os}>
        <:tab
          os={:linux}
          label="Linux"
          copy_text="curl -fsSL https://emisar.dev/install-mcp.sh | sudo bash -s -- --version <known-good-version>"
        ><span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> curl -fsSL https://emisar.dev/install-mcp.sh \
    | sudo bash -s -- --version &lt;known-good-version&gt;</:tab>
        <:tab
          os={:windows}
          label="Windows"
          copy_text={
            ~s|& ([scriptblock]::Create((irm https://emisar.dev/install-mcp.ps1))) -Version <known-good-version>|
          }
        ><span class="text-zinc-500">PS&gt;</span> &amp; ([scriptblock]::Create((irm https://emisar.dev/install-mcp.ps1))) `
    -Version &lt;known-good-version&gt;</:tab>
        <:tab
          os={:macos}
          label="macOS"
          copy_text="curl -fsSL https://emisar.dev/install-mcp.sh | sudo bash -s -- --version <known-good-version>"
        ><span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> curl -fsSL https://emisar.dev/install-mcp.sh \
    | sudo bash -s -- --version &lt;known-good-version&gt;</:tab>
      </.os_docs_code>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Only published releases install; a draft or still-changing release is refused. You can
        only go back as far as the minimum version above.
      </p>
    </.docs_layout>
  </section>

  <.marketing_footer />
</div>
